<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of the Heruli</h1><p class="st-meta">490 CE – 508 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived East Germanic kingdom on the middle Danube, briefly dominant over the Lombards before a crushing defeat around 508.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Heruli had been in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> region and under Hunnic rule; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>' collapse following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nedao">Battle of Nedao</a> in 454, one group settled near the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, probably in southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/great-moravia">Moravia</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>. By about 490 they had a strong kingdom that took tribute from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombards">Lombards</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> describes the Heruli as fierce warriors who kept old pagan customs, including the sacrifice of elderly people and the suicide of widows. Their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodulf-king-of-the-heruli">Rodulf</a> allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a>, who adopted him as a 'son in arms' around 507. The kingdom rested on warrior power and tribute rather than settled institutions.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 508 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodulf-king-of-the-heruli">Rodulf</a> attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> under Tato and was killed in a heavy defeat. The Heruli scattered: some moved into Roman territory and became federates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyricum-roman-province">Illyricum</a>, others went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>. Herul soldiers later fought for Justinian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-the-heruli.jpg" alt="Kingdom of the Heruli" width="320" height="266"><figcaption>Image: User:Andrein · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_125.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>490 CE – 508 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rodulf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Germanic paganism, later Arian Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>East Germanic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>, <a href="/countries/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Heruli</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=499&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-heruli" aria-label="Kingdom of the Heruli on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5010 512 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of the Heruli lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5218 839h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5330 768h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5330 768h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Austria. <a href="/atlas?year=499&amp;focus=kingdom-of-the-heruli">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

